point(20, "20-header-hygiene", "the public headers overflow into the program", "high", """
Review n° 22 (section 5). The public headers overflow into the program. gaol_interval_parser.h, installed, does not compile when included, and it and gaol_init_cleanup.h are internal; gaol_allocator.h does not compile alone. They put `using std::exception; using std::string;` at global scope and define macros without a prefix (INLINE, HAVE_FENV_H, MEMALIGN, __HI...). With -Wall -Wextra and a plain -I (pkg-config, or FetchContent, whose include directory is not SYSTEM), a program gets about 35 warnings: 30 -Wunused-parameter in gaol_expr_visitor.h, and a -Wdeprecated-copy at each `x = ...;` (a user-written copy constructor with an implicit copy assignment). Correction: stop installing the internal headers (in the three builds: the CMake install lists, Makefile.am, gaol/meson.build), remove the `using`s, prefix the macros, declare the copy assignment as default, leave the unused parameters unnamed. Method: (1) write a compile check that includes every installed header alone (one translation unit per header, with -Wall -Wextra -Wconversion -Wpedantic, as C++11 and C++17, GCC and Clang) and a program using GAOL with -Wall -Wextra: it must give zero warnings and leak no macro (list the macros with -dM -E before/after and compare with a list of allowed GAOL_-prefixed ones); make it a test of the suite (tests/fp_strict, tests/find_package and tests/fetch_content are examples of tests that build other programs), (2) fix. The internal headers may still be needed by installed ones (check the include graph; if an installed header includes an internal one, that one stays installed or the include goes). The macros are used all over gaol/*.cpp: renaming is mechanical but must not change behaviour (build all variants; the generated parser files include some macros: gaol_interval_parser.ypp/gaol_interval_lexer.lpp and their committed .cpp/.h must be regenerated with `cmake --build <build> --target parser` if you touch them: keep such edits to the minimum and say if you did). Removing the `using std::...` may break sources that relied on them: qualify with std::. ABI: no exported symbol may change (nm -D before/after on the shared library). Points n° 19 (the include structure of gaol_ieee1788.h and gaol_expression.h) and others touch these headers too: keep your edits to what your point needs.
""")

