# Interface
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
point(21, "21-integer-constructors", "integers beyond 2^53", "high", """
Integers beyond 2^53: `interval(0)`, `interval(0, 0)`, `x = 0`, `x < 0`, `max(x, 0)` and `T(0)` compile, GAOL v5 having no constructor from strings any more: the integer becomes a double. An integer beyond 2^53 thus becomes a double that does not contain it. Correction: constructor templates on the integer types, constrained (the point says it was checked on all the test programs and the sources of the library; in headers, with no ABI change). Not a plain interval(int), which makes interval(5L) ambiguous. Design: interval(n) for an integer n beyond 2^53 must CONTAIN the integer: the two neighbouring doubles, lower bound the largest double <= n and upper bound the smallest >= n, a point interval when n is representable. The same for the two-argument constructor (lower bound rounded down, upper bound rounded up), for assignment (x = n), for the comparisons and functions taking an integer (x < 0, max(x, 0): see which overloads exist, those taking a `double` today), and for T(0) in generic code. Mind the house rules: the constructors of interval are explicit and a double becomes an interval only where the program writes interval(d): `x = 0` and `x < 0` currently compile through some path: find out exactly which (grep operator=, the relation operators, the max/min overloads) and keep the set of accepted expressions the same, changing only what happens to big integers (do not make new expressions compile, do not stop old ones compiling). Care with signed/unsigned/long/long long/__int128 types, char and bool (do not accept bool and char by accident), enums, overload ambiguity with double and with the existing constructors, the ABI (templates in headers, no exported symbol changes) and C++11 (no if constexpr, no concepts: SFINAE with std::enable_if and std::is_integral). Tests in tests/constructor.cpp (and assignment.cpp, relations.cpp...): every integer type at 0, 1, -1, INT_MAX, LLONG_MAX, LLONG_MIN, ULLONG_MAX, 2^53, 2^53+1, 2^64-1: the interval contains the integer exactly (check with exact integer arithmetic, for instance long double or unsigned __int128 where available, or exact decimal strings with mpmath), and is a point interval when the integer is representable; static_asserts that interval(5L), interval(5u), interval(5ull) are unambiguous.
""")

point(22, "22-container-order", "no total order for containers", "high", """
No total order for containers. <, <=, > and >= are the "certainly" relations of IEEE 1788, true for (empty, empty): std::set loses the overlapping intervals, and std::sort of a vector containing an empty interval reads beyond its end. Correction: a gaol::lexicographic_less (perhaps a specialization of std::less: consider whether specializing std::less<gaol::interval> is wise: it is allowed for a user type and would make std::set<interval> and std::map work by default, but it changes silently what containers of intervals mean; decide and say why), and a warning in the documentation against std::sort, std::set, std::max, std::min and std::clamp without a comparator. Design: a strict weak ordering on intervals: compare the lower bounds, then the upper bounds, the empty set first (or last: choose, document); it must be a strict weak order including empty vs empty (equivalent) and -0.0 vs +0.0 (equivalent: compare with < on doubles), and no NaN problem: the empty set has NaN bounds in GAOL v5, so the comparator tests is_empty() explicitly. Header-only, public, documented in the Doxygen comments, doc/using.md (a section "Intervals in containers") and the manual, where the relations are described. Tests in tests/relations.cpp (or a suitable one): the strict weak order axioms checked exhaustively on a set of about 50 special intervals (irreflexivity, asymmetry, transitivity, transitivity of equivalence); std::sort of a vector containing empty intervals is correct under it; std::set<interval, gaol::lexicographic_less> keeps overlapping intervals; std::map works. The out-of-bounds read of std::sort with the raw operator< is undefined behaviour: show it in the documentation, not in the suite.
""")

point(23, "23-rounding-guard", "managing the rounding direction", "high", """
Managing the rounding direction. cleanup() restores it only once, there is no scoped guard, and rnd_keep() is not presented as a barrier. Correction: a gaol::restore_rounding() that can be called as many times as one wants, a guard that computes a block in round-to-nearest (about 7 ns), and rnd_keep() documented. Read gaol/gaol_init_cleanup.h, gaol_fpu.h and the documentation of init/cleanup (doc/using.md, manual/v5/gaol.tex; GAOL leaves the rounding direction upward between operations unless GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING; cleanup() restores the direction the program started with; every complete program in the docs ends with gaol::cleanup()). Design (say what you chose and why in your report): (a) gaol::restore_rounding(): sets back the direction that was in effect when GAOL initialized (the program's direction), idempotent, callable any number of times, cheap; what it does in GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING builds (a safe no-op: define it); the rounding mode is per thread: document; (b) a scope guard (RAII: for instance gaol::rounding_guard or gaol::scoped_nearest) whose constructor sets round-to-nearest (on x86 with SSE2 MXCSR only, consistently with how GAOL sets it) and whose destructor puts back upward (the state GAOL keeps), so that a block of the program's own floating-point code runs in nearest without leaking; nesting must work; an exception must not leak the state; (c) rnd_keep(): find what it is (grep: the barrier against compilers moving floating-point operations across a change of direction, see the notes on -frounding-math in gaol_fpu.h) and document it in doc/using.md and the manual as the barrier. Measure the cost of the guard (about 7 ns is the point's estimate) with clang-18 on a quiet machine; check that the direction is nearest inside and upward outside with fegetround() and _mm_getcsr(), in tests/rounding_direction.cpp (which already lists the states), also with GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING (a second build: -DGAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING=ON), with nesting, exceptions, and threads. Put the API in gaol_init_cleanup.h or the header where init/cleanup live; documented (Doxygen, doc/using.md, the manual).
""")

point(24, "24-fp-exceptions", "floating-point flags and exceptions", "low", """
Floating-point flags and exceptions. With hardware exceptions enabled (feenableexcept), 1/[0,1], log([0,1]), [1e308]*10 and every is_empty() of a computed empty set die on SIGFPE: the unbounded results come from overflows or divisions by zero, and is_empty() compares NaN bounds with a signaling comparison. Every operation raises the inexact flag. Correction: is_empty() with std::islessequal, at no cost, and a word in the documentation saying that hardware exceptions must be disabled. Find every place that decides emptiness with a comparison on NaN bounds (is_empty() inline in gaol/gaol_interval.h, its overloads, the comparisons in gaol_interval_sse.cpp/gaol_interval_fpu.cpp: grep isnan, is_empty, `!(`), and make the ones the point names use std::islessequal (or an equivalent quiet comparison) at no measurable cost (compare the assembly of is_empty() before and after with clang-18 -O3 and gcc -O3; micro-benchmark on a quiet machine); is_empty() is inline in a header: no ABI change. Test: a program (tests/, POSIX/glibc only: guard with the availability of feenableexcept; run the check in a child process (fork) or with a SIGFPE handler and sigsetjmp so that without the fix the test fails cleanly instead of killing the runner; Windows and macOS skip and the test says so) that enables FE_INVALID, computes empty sets (an empty intersection, sqrt of a negative interval, [1,2] & [3,4], interval::emptyset()) and calls is_empty() on them, in both builds sse and fpu. Documentation (doc/using.md, doc/accuracy.md if it fits, manual/v5/gaol.tex): hardware floating-point exceptions must be disabled while GAOL computes (1/[0,1], log([0,1]) and [1e308]*10 raise divide-by-zero or overflow legitimately) and every operation raises the inexact flag (fetestexcept() cannot detect anything about GAOL results). Do not try to make the other operations trap-free: that is the documentation part of the point.
""")

